+### 13. Name the revision by its release label
+
+An owner reads "Butlers v1.0.23" faster than a 40-digit object id. Polaris
+leads with the label and keeps the object id beneath it, because a tag can be
+moved and the object id cannot.
+
+- **Inputs.** Two deterministic evaluation inputs, each with its own
+  identity, are taken at the capture instant:
+  - the captured tag set: every ref under `refs/tags/`, each with the object
+    it peels to;
+  - the captured ancestry: the parent ids of the commits in the revision's
+    history, complete only when every one was read.
+
+  Ref names and object ids are all that is taken. Tag messages, signatures
+  and commit messages are never read into the model.
+- **Form.** The first applicable test names the revision:
+  - not read, when the tag set was not captured: "release tags were not
+    read";
+  - tagged, when a tag peels to the revision;
+  - not read, when the ancestry is incomplete: "the history needed to judge
+    release tags was not read";
+  - described, when the nearest reaching tag is some commits behind;
+  - untagged, when no tag reaches the revision: "no release tag reaches this
+    revision".
+
+  "Not read" is never shown as "untagged": an absence nobody looked for is
+  Unknown (VIS-2). Each not-read form says which read is missing, so neither
+  statement is false when the other holds.
+- **Label unit.** The revision is named only in a marked unit: the label,
+  its statement, the full object id beneath, the expansion of tied names and
+  any moved-tag disclosure. Every Polaris page, meaning `/polaris` and each
+  exact-source route response, shows at least one, and on each page the
+  first visible id of the revision, the shared status line aside, is in a
+  unit, with the label before the id. A checker counts the units by their
+  marks, then sweeps the visible text for any other short or full id of the
+  revision, or project name followed by a tag name. Four marked kinds are
+  excepted, and the checker decides each from what it reads, not from the
+  mark:
+  - a per-claim provenance stamp, which is only the revision element of a
+    citation whose cite names a served anchor or source record carrying
+    that revision, so a sentence or heading never holds one;
+  - an identity shown whole, equal to the evaluation identity or a served
+    record's own identity;
+  - Butlers-declared text kept verbatim;
+  - the shared operability status line, which no requirement of either
+    specification names.
+
+  Link targets are left to the tag-name sweep.
+- **Binding.** Claims, evaluation identities, links and comparisons bind to
+  the object id. An evaluation names the earlier evaluations it is compared
+  with as inputs; a tag one of them bound to another commit is disclosed as
+  moved, naming both commits, wherever its name is carried, tied names
+  included.
+- **Read authority.** Reading the tag set and counting commits back to a tag
+  read Git refs and commit ancestry. The observer's registry entry does not
+  admit either today, so until a registry amendment does, every label takes
+  the not-read form.
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart TD
+    T{"Tag set captured?"} -- "no" --> NT["Not read: short id,<br/>'release tags were not read',<br/>reason and route; never 'untagged'"]
+    T -- "yes" --> Z{"A tag peels to<br/>the revision?"}
+    Z -- "yes" --> TG["Tagged: Butlers v1.0.23"]
+    Z -- "no" --> H{"Ancestry<br/>complete?"}
+    H -- "no" --> NH["Not read: short id,<br/>'the history needed to judge<br/>release tags was not read',<br/>reason and route; never 'untagged'"]
+    H -- "yes" --> A{"A tag reaches it<br/>from an ancestor?"}
+    A -- "yes" --> DS["Described: nearest tag<br/>plus N later commits"]
+    A -- "no" --> UT["Untagged: short id,<br/>no release tag reaches it"]
+```
+
 ## Risks / Trade-offs
 
 Each known risk has a fail-closed answer; the one residual risk the owner
